-- 0035 — seed for place_profiles (see 0033 for why this is a seed and not a runtime write).
--
-- 361 profiles, generated by scripts/build-place-profiles.cjs with claude-sonnet-4-6,
-- using the SAME system prompt /api/base-info uses, so a cached profile and a
-- live one cannot read like different products.
--
-- APPLYING THIS MIGRATION IS THE ACT OF REVIEW. Read
-- docs/place-profiles-review.md first — that is the same text, laid out to be
-- read rather than parsed. These rows land reviewed = true and are served
-- immediately; a profile found to be wrong later is pulled from circulation
-- with `update place_profiles set reviewed = false where name_key = '...'`,
-- which falls that place back to the live model call rather than deleting
-- anything.

insert into place_profiles (name_key, display_name, profile, model, reviewed, reviewed_at) values
  ('al asad air base', 'Al Asad Air Base', 'Al Asad Air Base sits in the vast Anbar desert of western Iraq, roughly 180 kilometers west of Baghdad. The base originated as an Iraqi Air Force installation built in the 1980s during the Iran-Iraq War, and by the time coalition forces arrived in 2003, it had grown into one of the largest air bases in the country — a sprawling complex of hardened aircraft shelters, long runways, and bunkers that told the story of Saddam-era military ambition.

American forces seized and expanded Al Asad significantly after the 2003 invasion, and it became a major hub for Marine Corps operations across Anbar Province — one of the most contested regions of the Iraq War. Fixed-wing aircraft, helicopters, and logistics operations all flowed through its runways, supporting campaigns in Fallujah, Ramadi, and the vast desert stretches toward the Syrian border. At its peak it was home to tens of thousands of personnel and functioned almost like a small city dropped into the scrubland.

The base saw continued use well into subsequent phases of the conflict, including operations against ISIS, when it once again became a forward anchor for coalition efforts in the region.

Those who served at Al Asad carried a hard mission in hard country, and they carried it with professionalism.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('camp taji', 'Camp Taji', 'Camp Taji sits about 20 miles north of Baghdad on the flat, sun-baked plain between the Tigris River and the agricultural heartland of central Iraq. The site dates back decades before American forces arrived — Saddam Hussein''s military developed it into one of Iraq''s largest military-industrial complexes, housing armor depots, ammunition storage, and weapons manufacturing across thousands of acres. Coalition forces seized and repurposed it after the 2003 invasion, and it became a major U.S. and Iraqi Army installation almost immediately.

During the Iraq War, Taji served as a logistics hub, training ground, and forward base for units rotating through central Iraq. Soldiers, Marines, and contractors worked alongside Iraqi security forces there, particularly as the mission shifted toward advising and building partner capacity. The base was large enough to feel like a small city — with flight lines, motor pools, and the particular dust that got into everything.

In later years, Taji remained a key site for training Iraqi forces, and it saw renewed attention during operations against ISIS after 2014.

Those who served at Taji kept a critical piece of Iraq running — and their work there mattered long after they left.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('joint base balad (anaconda)', 'Joint Base Balad (Anaconda)', 'Joint Base Balad, known to most who served there as Logistical Support Area Anaconda, sits in the Sunni Triangle roughly sixty miles north of Baghdad, near the city of Balad in Salah ad-Din Province. The airfield there dates to the Iraqi Air Force under Saddam Hussein''s government, who built it as a major aviation installation before American forces seized and repurposed it in the opening weeks of the 2003 invasion.

At its peak, LSA Anaconda was one of the largest American bases in Iraq — home to tens of thousands of personnel at a time, earning the nickname "Mortaritaville" for the near-constant indirect fire it absorbed throughout the insurgency years. It served as a massive logistics and air hub, with the airfield supporting everything from C-130 resupply runs to medevac operations and combat sorties. Soldiers, airmen, contractors, and coalition partners all shared its dusty roads and hardened bunkers.

Despite the rockets and mortars, daily life there had an almost surreal quality — fast food trailers, a large PX, and the constant roar of aircraft overhead, all surrounded by concertina wire and the flat Iraqi plain stretching to every horizon.

Those who kept that base running kept the whole mission alive.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('camp victory / baghdad (biap)', 'Camp Victory / Baghdad (BIAP)', 'Camp Victory sat on the western edge of Baghdad, built around one of Saddam Hussein''s most opulent palace complexes on the shores of an artificial lake near Baghdad International Airport. The Americans seized the site in April 2003 and transformed it almost overnight from a symbol of the old regime into the nerve center of an entirely new war. The sprawling compound eventually became one of the largest U.S. military installations outside the continental United States.

For nearly a decade, Victory served as the headquarters of Multi-National Force–Iraq and later U.S. Forces–Iraq, coordinating operations across the entire country. Soldiers, Marines, airmen, sailors, and civilians from dozens of coalition nations rotated through its gates. The Al-Faw Palace at its center — marble floors, chandeliers, and all — became a military headquarters that no one could quite believe they were working inside.

At its peak, tens of thousands of personnel lived and worked across the Victory Base Complex, a cluster of interconnected camps that felt, to many, like a small American city transplanted into the desert heat of central Iraq.

Those who served there carried the weight of an entire war on their shoulders, and they bore it.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('camp speicher (tikrit)', 'Camp Speicher (Tikrit)', 'Camp Speicher sat on the outskirts of Tikrit, Iraq, on a broad stretch of terrain along the Tigris River that Saddam Hussein had once claimed as his home ground. The base took its name from Navy Lieutenant Commander Scott Speicher, the first American servicemember killed in the Gulf War, shot down over Iraq in January 1991 — a name carried with quiet weight by everyone who passed through the gate.

Through the Iraq War, Speicher served as a major logistics and aviation hub in Saladin Province, supporting operations across a volatile region where insurgent activity ran deep. It was home to rotating Army brigades, aviation units, and supporting elements whose work ranged from combat patrols to reconstruction efforts in the surrounding towns and villages. The airfield kept a steady tempo — helicopters and fixed-wing aircraft moving troops and supplies across the north.

The base was large enough to feel like a small city, yet the mission never let anyone forget where they were. Tikrit was contested ground, and everyone stationed or transiting through understood what that meant.

Those who served at Camp Speicher carried a hard assignment with professionalism and steadiness — and that matters.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('mosul (camp marez / diamondback)', 'Mosul (Camp Marez / Diamondback)', 'Mosul sits at a bend in the Tigris River in northern Iraq, one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities on earth — built near the ruins of ancient Nineveh, capital of the Assyrian Empire. When U.S. forces moved into the area in 2003, they established two adjoining installations: Camp Marez and FOB Diamondback, positioned near the Mosul airport on the city''s western edge. The names and boundaries shifted over the years, but the mission stayed consistent — securing a complex, ethnically mixed city where Arabs, Kurds, Turkmen, and Christians lived in uneasy proximity.

The installations supported a rotating cast of Army units conducting counterinsurgency operations, training Iraqi security forces, and patrolling some of the most contested urban terrain of the war. Mosul was never quiet. The city''s size, its proximity to Syria, and its deep political fault lines made it a persistent pressure point throughout the occupation.

Camp Marez is also remembered for one of the deadliest single incidents of the Iraq War, a 2004 dining facility attack that killed more than twenty people — a moment that hit the force hard and stayed with everyone who was there.

Those who served in Mosul held the line in a city that never made it easy — and they did it with discipline and persistence, day after day.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('camp ramadi', 'Camp Ramadi', 'Camp Ramadi sat in the heart of Al Anbar Province, Iraq, on the western outskirts of Ramadi — the provincial capital and, for a significant stretch of the Iraq War, one of the most contested cities in the country. The base served as a central hub for U.S. Army and Marine Corps forces operating in a region where insurgent activity was among the heaviest anywhere in Iraq, particularly between 2004 and 2007.

Units rotating through Camp Ramadi took on the grinding work of urban counterinsurgency — patrolling densely packed neighborhoods, working with Iraqi Security Forces, and supporting the broader effort to wrest Al Anbar from insurgent control. The Anbar Awakening, the pivotal movement in which Sunni tribal leaders turned against Al Qaeda in Iraq, took shape in and around this city, and the soldiers and Marines at Camp Ramadi were on the ground as that shift unfolded.

The base itself was austere and exposed, close enough to the city that the war was never abstract — it came to the wire regularly. Serving there demanded steadiness under real and persistent pressure.

Those who stood watch at Camp Ramadi did so on some of the hardest ground of a hard war, and they held it.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('camp fallujah', 'Camp Fallujah', 'Camp Fallujah sat on the eastern edge of one of the most contested cities in Iraq, built on the grounds of a former Iraqi military compound that American forces seized and repurposed after the 2003 invasion. Its location was no accident — Fallujah sat astride key routes in Al Anbar Province, and whoever controlled the ground around it controlled access to a significant stretch of central Iraq.

The camp served as a major command and logistics hub for Marine Corps and coalition forces operating in Al Anbar throughout the mid-2000s. It supported the two major battles for Fallujah in 2004, particularly Operation Phantom Fury in November — one of the most intense urban battles American forces had fought since Vietnam. Units rotated through, planned, rested, and launched from its perimeter while the city beyond the wire remained volatile for years.

Life there meant the constant awareness that the wire was close, the city was closer, and the mission never really paused. It was a working base in a working war, with all the dust, noise, and purpose that came with that.

Those who served at Camp Fallujah stood watch over one of the hardest pieces of ground American forces were asked to hold — and they held it.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('camp bucca', 'Camp Bucca', 'Camp Bucca sat in the desert of southern Iraq, near the Kuwaiti border, established by coalition forces in the early days of the 2003 invasion. It was named for Ronald Bucca, a New York City Fire Marshal and Army Reserve soldier who died at the World Trade Center on September 11, 2001 — a name chosen to carry meaning from the beginning.

The facility grew into one of the largest detention operations in the Iraq War, holding tens of thousands of detainees at its peak and drawing military police, intelligence personnel, and support units from across the Army and other branches. Managing that population — in brutal desert heat, across a sprawling compound of wire and guard towers — was grinding, relentless work that demanded patience and professionalism every single day.

What happened inside those fences mattered far beyond the wire. The camp''s population and the complex dynamics within it shaped events that echoed across the broader conflict in ways that are still studied today.

Those who kept watch there, in one of the most demanding detention environments American forces have ever operated, did so with discipline the mission demanded and the country required.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('tallil air base (camp adder)', 'Tallil Air Base (Camp Adder)', 'Tallil Air Base sits in southern Iraq near the ancient city of Ur, one of the oldest continuously inhabited places on earth. The base traces its origins to the Iraqi Air Force, which developed it as a major installation during the Iran-Iraq War in the 1980s. Coalition forces seized the airfield during Operation Desert Storm in 1991, and it became a significant staging point for American and allied operations. After 2003, the United States expanded it substantially, and it took on the additional name Camp Adder — a name used by the Army component sharing the sprawling complex.

At its peak during the Iraq War, Tallil hosted Air Force fighter and airlift units, Army logistics and support brigades, and a rotating cast of coalition partners. The 407th Air Expeditionary Group flew missions from its runways, and the base served as a critical hub for moving personnel and materiel across theater.

What made Tallil genuinely striking was the view: the ancient ziggurat of Ur — over four thousand years old — rose from the flat desert just beyond the perimeter wire, a daily reminder of how deep history ran beneath that ground.

Those who served at Tallil stood watch over some of the oldest earth civilization ever called home.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('kirkuk air base', 'Kirkuk Air Base', 'Kirkuk Air Base sits on the ancient Assyrian plain of northern Iraq, a place where oil derricks and dust devils share the horizon with ruins that predate written history. The base traces its modern military origins to the Iraqi Air Force, which operated it for decades before American forces arrived. After the 2003 invasion, coalition troops — including U.S. Army and Air Force personnel — established a significant presence here, using it as a critical hub for operations across the volatile northern region. The base supported airlift, intelligence, and combat operations during some of the most complex years of the Iraq War.

What set Kirkuk apart was its setting: a contested, ethnically layered city just beyond the wire, claimed by Kurds, Arabs, and Turkmen alike, making the political terrain as demanding as the military one. Soldiers and airmen operated in an environment where tribal dynamics, oil politics, and insurgent activity overlapped constantly.

Those who served at Kirkuk carried a mission that required as much patience and judgment as it did strength — and they carried it with professionalism.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('q-west (qayyarah west)', 'Q-West (Qayyarah West)', 'Qayyarah West Airfield — Q-West to everyone who served there — sits in northern Iraq along the Tigris River, roughly 50 miles south of Mosul. The airfield predates the American presence by decades, built by the Iraqi military and used by Saddam Hussein''s air force. Its location made it strategically obvious: control Q-West and you hold a critical corridor into the northern part of the country.

American forces seized and activated the base in 2003 during the initial push into Iraq, and it became a steady logistics and aviation hub supporting operations across Nineveh Province and the surrounding region. Various Army units cycled through over the years, using it to push supplies, launch patrols, and maintain reach into some of the war''s more demanding terrain. The base sat in open, dusty lowlands — flat, exposed, and hot in summer — with the river providing one of the few natural landmarks in an otherwise austere landscape.

Q-West came back into use years later when coalition forces returned to the region to confront ISIS, again serving as a platform for operations around Mosul.

Those who pulled duty at Q-West — in any rotation — kept a critical piece of northern Iraq functioning when it mattered most.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('khamisiyah', 'Khamisiyah', 'Khamisiyah is a name most veterans of the Gulf War era will recognize — a sprawling ammunition storage complex in southern Iraq, near the town of An Nasiriyah in the Euphrates River valley. The site had served as a major depot for the Iraqi military, one of the largest of its kind in the country, built up over years to support Saddam Hussein''s armed forces.

In the days following the ground campaign''s end in early March 1991, American combat engineers — primarily from the 37th Engineer Battalion — demolished munitions stockpiles at Khamisiyah as part of post-combat clearing operations. The scale of the site was immense: bunkers and open pits spread across a wide stretch of flat, featureless desert that offered little shade and no cover from the wind.

The demolitions were carried out in good faith by soldiers doing exactly what they were ordered and trained to do. The work was hard, unglamorous, and conducted in the silence that follows a war.

Those who worked the ground at Khamisiyah served without hesitation, in the aftermath of victory, when the hardest jobs still remained.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('camp arifjan', 'Camp Arifjan', 'Camp Arifjan sits in the Kuwaiti desert south of Kuwait City, on ground that became strategically vital to the United States military in the aftermath of the Gulf War. Established in the 1990s as a permanent U.S. Army installation, it was built to ensure a sustained American presence in the region and to serve as a reliable logistics hub close to potential flashpoints in the Gulf.

The camp became the beating heart of Army sustainment operations during Operation Iraqi Freedom and Operation Enduring Freedom, processing hundreds of thousands of troops and mountains of equipment flowing in and out of theater. It served as headquarters for U.S. Army Central — ARCENT — and hosted soldiers, civilians, and coalition partners from across the joint force. For many, Arifjan was both a first step into the fight and the last stop before heading home.

Life on Arifjan was shaped by the flat, sun-scorched Kuwaiti landscape, relentless heat, and the particular rhythm of a major rear-area post — busy, logistically intense, and always moving. It was never glamorous work, but it kept the mission alive.

To every soldier who moved through that desert installation, your service kept the force in the fight.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('ali al salem air base', 'Ali Al Salem Air Base', 'Ali Al Salem Air Base sits in the Kuwaiti desert northwest of Kuwait City, on terrain that is flat, sun-blasted, and unforgiving in summer. The base traces its origins to the Kuwaiti Air Force and carries the name of a member of the Al-Sabah royal family. Its runway and facilities were seized by Iraqi forces during the 1990 invasion and then recaptured and rebuilt by coalition forces following Desert Storm — a transformation that would define its next three decades of purpose.

From the 1990s onward, Ali Al Salem became one of the most consistently active American air bases in the region. It served as a critical hub for operations enforcing the southern no-fly zone over Iraq, and later as a major launching point for missions during Operation Iraqi Freedom and the long sustainment operations that followed. The Air Force ran flight operations here continuously across multiple generations of airmen, and ground forces transited through on their way in and out of theater.

The base is known for its heat, its dust, and the relentless operational tempo that kept crews turning aircraft around at all hours.

Those who served at Ali Al Salem kept a vital door open in one of the world''s most consequential neighborhoods — and they did it with professionalism through year after year of quiet, demanding work.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('camp buehring (udairi)', 'Camp Buehring (Udairi)', 'Camp Buehring sits in the Kuwaiti desert near the Iraqi border, on terrain that is flat, sun-blasted, and almost featureless — a landscape that has a way of focusing the mind on what lies ahead. The camp was established in the early 2000s to support the buildup of forces before the 2003 invasion of Iraq, and it was named for Lieutenant Colonel Charles H. Buehring, an Army officer killed in a rocket-propelled grenade attack in Baghdad in October 2003. Renaming the installation in his honor gave the place a human anchor in an otherwise austere setting.

For soldiers and Marines rotating through, Buehring was the last significant stop before crossing into Iraq — a place to draw equipment, run ranges, acclimate to the heat, and make sure everything and everyone was ready. It served as a major staging and training hub throughout Operation Iraqi Freedom and Operation New Dawn, processing hundreds of thousands of troops over more than a decade. It also became a key waypoint for units rotating out, which gave it a different kind of weight on the return trip.

Those who passed through Buehring carried the mission forward — and the ground remembers every one of them.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('camp doha', 'Camp Doha', 'Camp Doha sat on the outskirts of Kuwait City, a sprawling logistics and staging base that became one of the most consequential American military footprints in the Middle East. Established in the early 1990s in the aftermath of Operation Desert Storm, it was built to ensure the U.S. could respond rapidly if Iraq threatened the region again — a lesson learned hard from 1990.

For more than a decade, Doha served as the Army''s primary prepositioning site in the Gulf, warehousing heavy equipment, ammunition, and vehicles so that forces could flow in and fight without waiting weeks for ships. When Operation Iraqi Freedom launched in 2003, Doha hummed around the clock — a nerve center for logistics, command coordination, and the movement of troops and materiel northward into Iraq.

The base sat in the flat Kuwaiti desert, relentlessly hot, with the smell of sand and diesel in the air. For many soldiers, it was the last stop before crossing the berm, or the first breath of relative safety on the way home.

Those who served at Doha kept the machine running — and that work mattered every mile north.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('al udeid air base', 'Al Udeid Air Base', 'Al Udeid Air Base sits on the desert flats southwest of Doha, Qatar, a landscape of sand and scorching heat that makes the air shimmer even at dawn. Qatar and the United States developed the base in partnership during the 1990s, and it grew rapidly after 2001 into one of the most consequential American air installations in the world. The base houses the forward headquarters of U.S. Air Forces Central and has served as a hub for operations across Afghanistan, Iraq, Syria, and the broader Middle East.

At its peak, Al Udeid hosted tens of thousands of personnel from the Air Force, Army, Navy, and partner nations, running around-the-clock flight operations from one of the longest runways in the region. Tanker crews flew refueling missions that kept strike aircraft aloft for hours. Intelligence and command-and-control teams coordinated some of the most complex air campaigns in American history. The heat was relentless, the pace unforgiving, and the stakes real every single day.

Those who served at Al Udeid kept the air war running — and that work mattered.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('al dhafra air base', 'Al Dhafra Air Base', 'Al Dhafra Air Base sits in the desert southwest of Abu Dhabi, in the United Arab Emirates — a place where the flat, sun-hammered terrain stretches toward Saudi Arabia and the air itself seems to shimmer year-round. The UAE military operates the base, but American forces have maintained a significant presence there since at least the 1990s, with the U.S. Air Force using it as a critical hub for operations across the broader Middle East and Central Asia.

Over the decades, Al Dhafra supported combat and intelligence missions during Operations Southern Watch, Enduring Freedom, and Iraqi Freedom, among others. The base became home to some of the Air Force''s most advanced aircraft — including F-22s, F-35s, U-2s, and aerial refueling tankers — making it one of the most capable air installations in the region. Its long runways and strategic location made it indispensable for projecting airpower across an enormous swath of the world.

Daily life there meant operating in intense heat, navigating a joint and coalition environment, and sustaining missions that rarely made headlines but mattered enormously to the fight.

Those who served at Al Dhafra kept watch over a volatile region, and their professionalism reflected well on every uniform they wore.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('naval support activity bahrain', 'Naval Support Activity Bahrain', 'Naval Support Activity Bahrain sits on a small man-made peninsula in Manama, the capital of the Kingdom of Bahrain, and its roots go back to World War II, when the U.S. Navy first established a presence in the Persian Gulf. The base grew into a permanent installation during the Cold War era and became the homeport of U.S. Naval Forces Central Command — making it the Navy''s nerve center for one of the world''s most strategically critical waterways.

The installation has supported virtually every major American naval operation in the Middle East for decades, from escort missions during the Tanker War of the 1980s through Desert Storm, the ongoing patrol of the Strait of Hormuz, and sustained operations supporting both Iraq and Afghanistan. The Fifth Fleet is headquartered here, coordinating a vast area of responsibility stretching from the Red Sea to the Arabian Sea.

Life on NSA Bahrain means operating in intense heat, in close quarters with coalition partners from across the globe, and with the Persian Gulf always nearby — a reminder of why the mission matters. The sailors, officers, and civilians who have served there have kept open some of the most consequential sea lanes on earth.

Those who stood watch in Bahrain carried a quiet, consequential responsibility that the world rarely sees — and never fully appreciates.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('prince sultan air base', 'Prince Sultan Air Base', 'Prince Sultan Air Base sits in the stark central Arabian desert, roughly sixty miles southeast of Riyadh, on terrain that is flat, pale, and punishing — summer heat routinely exceeds 110 degrees, and the emptiness stretches in every direction. The base traces its modern military significance to the Gulf War era, when American forces began operating from Saudi soil in earnest, and it grew into one of the largest and most capable U.S. air installations in the Middle East through the 1990s and into the post-September 11th period.

For more than a decade, it served as a hub for Air Force operations enforcing the southern no-fly zone over Iraq — Operation Southern Watch — with thousands of airmen rotating through, flying missions day and night over some of the world''s most watched airspace. Fighter wings, tankers, AWACS crews, and support personnel all called it home in turns. The base was named for a senior Saudi royal, reflecting the partnership that made American presence there possible.

Life on the compound meant dust, heat, strict general orders, and an acute sense of purpose far from home.

Those who served there held a line that mattered, quietly and professionally, in one of the most consequential regions on earth.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('eskan village', 'Eskan Village', 'Eskan Village is a residential compound on the southern edge of Riyadh, Saudi Arabia, originally built by the Saudi government as housing for nomadic tribes being resettled into urban life. When the Gulf War began in 1990, the compound was made available to U.S. and coalition forces, and it became one of the primary billeting areas for American personnel flowing into the kingdom during Operations Desert Shield and Desert Storm.

Over the years that followed, Eskan took on a longer-term role as a support and administrative hub for U.S. Air Force and other coalition personnel operating in the region. It housed various headquarters elements and support units through the long years of enforcement operations over Iraq and into the post-9/11 era. Life there was defined by a strange contrast — the compound''s low-rise buildings and landscaped grounds sat quietly inside one of the world''s largest cities, a small American footprint in an ancient and sovereign land.

The compound was known for its isolation from the surrounding city, which made it both a refuge and a reminder of just how far from home the mission really was.

Those who served at Eskan Village carried a quiet, essential burden — far from the front, never far from the mission.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('bagram airfield', 'Bagram Airfield', 'Bagram Airfield sits in the Shomali Plain north of Kabul, cradled by the Hindu Kush, at roughly 4,900 feet elevation. The base traces its origins to the 1950s, when the Soviet Union helped build the airfield as part of Cold War–era development aid to Afghanistan. Soviet forces expanded and operated it heavily during their occupation in the 1980s, and the scars of that conflict — old minefields, wrecked aircraft — were still visible when American forces arrived in late 2001.

After the fall of the Taliban, Bagram became the largest U.S. military installation in Afghanistan and the operational hub for everything that followed — combat missions, logistics, special operations, medevac, intelligence work, and the steady rhythm of rotations that cycled hundreds of thousands of American service members through its gates. At its peak, the base housed tens of thousands of personnel from across all branches and from coalition nations around the world.

The mountains surrounding Bagram were not backdrop — they were ever-present, defining the weather, the flight patterns, and the tactical reality of every mission that launched from that runway.

To everyone who served at Bagram, in any capacity, any era — your service on that ground mattered.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('kandahar airfield', 'Kandahar Airfield', 'Kandahar Airfield sits in the flatlands of southern Afghanistan, a place that has seen conquerors and caravans for thousands of years. The airfield itself was built during the Soviet-Afghan War era and later fell into disrepair through the civil war of the 1990s. After the fall of the Taliban in late 2001, coalition forces quickly seized and rebuilt it, transforming a battered strip of tarmac into one of the largest and busiest military airfields in the world.

At its peak during the Afghan War, KAF was home to tens of thousands of troops from the United States, Canada, the United Kingdom, the Netherlands, Australia, and dozens of other nations. It served as the hub of operations across Regional Command South — the spiritual and strategic heartland of the Taliban. Special operations units, conventional infantry, aviators, intelligence teams, and logistics personnel all flowed through its gates.

Life at KAF had its own strange texture: the infamous "poo pond," the boardwalk with its makeshift shops, and the constant rumble of C-130s and attack helicopters overhead. Despite its size, it was never far from the war.

Those who served there carried a heavy mission on hard ground, and they carried it with honor.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('fob salerno (khost)', 'FOB Salerno (Khost)', 'FOB Salerno sat in Khost Province in southeastern Afghanistan, pressed against the Pakistan border in one of the most contested corridors of the war. The base took its name from the World War II Allied amphibious landing at Salerno, Italy, in September 1943 — a reminder that American soldiers have been fighting their way onto difficult ground for generations. Khost itself had been a flashpoint long before 2001; the region''s tribal networks and border terrain made it strategically important and persistently dangerous.

Salerno served as a major hub for operations across Khost and into Paktia and Paktika provinces, supporting Army infantry, Special Forces, and coalition units throughout the post-2001 campaign. Soldiers stationed there ran patrols into rugged mountain villages, conducted counterinsurgency missions, and absorbed regular rocket and mortar attacks — earning the base the grim nickname "Rocket City." The perimeter was tested repeatedly, including a complex attack in 2012 that struck hard at the installation itself.

Life at Salerno meant heat, dust, altitude, and constant vigilance in a province that never fully quieted down.

Those who served at Salerno stood watch on some of the hardest ground in Afghanistan, and they held it.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('camp phoenix (kabul)', 'Camp Phoenix (Kabul)', 'Camp Phoenix sat on the eastern edge of Kabul, established in the early years of the post-2001 mission in Afghanistan to serve as a central hub for the training and mentorship of the Afghan National Army. The name carried intent — this was a place built around the idea of rebuilding something from the ground up, standing a new force on its feet in a country that had known little but war for a generation.

The camp became home to the Combined Security Transition Command and rotations of National Guard and Reserve soldiers from across the United States, many of them on their first deployment. Embedded Training Teams and later Security Force Assistance teams worked daily alongside Afghan counterparts, a grind of patience, repetition, and relationship-building that rarely made headlines but mattered enormously to the mission''s long-term design.

Phoenix was never a remote outpost — Kabul surrounded it — which gave the work a particular intensity. The city was always present, audible, and real.

Those who served at Camp Phoenix gave their time to one of the harder tasks in modern warfare: not fighting alone, but teaching others to stand and fight for themselves.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('jalalabad airfield (fob fenty)', 'Jalalabad Airfield (FOB Fenty)', 'Jalalabad Airfield sits in the Nangarhar Province of eastern Afghanistan, in a broad valley where the Kunar and Kabul rivers meet near the Pakistani border. The airfield itself predates American involvement by decades — it was built during the Soviet-Afghan War era and used by various factions through Afghanistan''s long years of conflict. U.S. forces established a presence there shortly after the fall of the Taliban in late 2001, and the installation was eventually named FOB Fenty in honor of Colonel Joseph Fenty, a 10th Mountain Division officer killed in a helicopter crash in Kunar Province in 2006.

The base served as a critical hub for operations across one of Afghanistan''s most contested corridors. Units from the 10th Mountain Division, 101st Airborne, special operations forces, and coalition partners cycled through, supporting counterinsurgency operations, advising Afghan security forces, and running missions into the rugged valleys stretching toward the Khyber Pass and beyond. Medevac and intelligence assets operated continuously from its flight line.

The terrain defined everything here — mountains rising sharply on all sides, heat in summer, and a proximity to the border that kept the mission relentless.

To everyone who served at Fenty, your work in that valley mattered.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('fob sharana (paktika)', 'FOB Sharana (Paktika)', 'FOB Sharana sat in the heart of Paktika Province in southeastern Afghanistan, a rugged stretch of high desert and mountain terrain that shares a long, porous border with Pakistan. The base grew from a small outpost into a significant forward operating base during the surge years of the Afghan War, serving as the headquarters for Provincial Reconstruction Team Paktika and a hub for combat operations across one of the country''s most active insurgent corridors.

Soldiers stationed there — primarily Army units rotating through — ran patrols into some of Afghanistan''s most unforgiving country, coordinating with local Afghan security forces while also supporting reconstruction and governance efforts across the province. The base lay at roughly 7,000 feet elevation, meaning thin air, hard winters, and distances that tested every vehicle and every body.

Paktika was never a famous front, but it was a real one. The troops who served at Sharana did steady, grinding work far from headlines — holding ground, building relationships, and keeping pressure on networks that used the border region as a lifeline.

Those who served at FOB Sharana did their duty in a hard place, and that counts for something lasting.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('camp leatherneck / bastion', 'Camp Leatherneck / Bastion', 'Camp Leatherneck and Camp Bastion sat together in the open desert of Helmand Province, Afghanistan — a place where the ground is flat, the wind carries grit, and the distances are humbling. The British established Camp Bastion in 2006 as their main operating base in Helmand, and the United States built Camp Leatherneck adjacent to it beginning in 2008, creating one of the largest military installations ever constructed in Afghanistan. Together they formed a sprawling complex covering roughly twenty-seven square miles — a small city risen from nothing in the middle of the desert.

The base served as the hub for Marine Corps operations across Helmand throughout the surge years, supporting major offensives into Taliban strongholds like Marjah and Sangin. At its peak, tens of thousands of coalition personnel from the U.S., U.K., and partner nations operated from its runways, flight lines, and forward staging areas. The air operations alone were extraordinary in scale and tempo.

Marines handed Leatherneck back to Afghan forces in 2014, closing a chapter defined by relentless effort in some of the hardest terrain of the war.

Those who served on that desert ground carried the mission with grit and professionalism the Corps has always demanded.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('camp dwyer (helmand)', 'Camp Dwyer (Helmand)', 'Camp Dwyer sat in the open desert of Helmand Province, Afghanistan, on terrain so flat and exposed that the wind came at you from every direction and the summer heat was punishing even by Afghan standards. The camp was named for First Lieutenant Joseph Dwyer, a U.S. Army medic whose photograph — carrying a wounded Iraqi child to safety during the 2003 invasion — became one of the defining images of that war. Naming a forward base for him carried a quiet weight: a reminder that care and courage belong together.

The installation grew significantly during the surge years, serving as a key logistics and command hub for Marine Corps operations throughout southern Helmand. It supported missions pushing into some of the most contested ground in the country — districts where coalition and Afghan forces fought hard to establish any durable security. Helicopters moved constantly in and out, and the base became a critical link in the chain keeping Marines and sailors supplied and supported across a sprawling, dangerous province.

Those who served at Dwyer carried a demanding mission in an unforgiving place, and they carried it with professionalism.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('shindand air base (herat)', 'Shindand Air Base (Herat)', 'Shindand Air Base sits in the Farah River valley of western Afghanistan, in Herat Province, roughly midway between the Iranian border and the city of Herat. The Soviets built and heavily developed the base during their occupation in the 1980s, and it became one of the largest air installations in the country — a fact that made it strategically significant long before American boots arrived. Its sprawling runways and Soviet-era infrastructure told the story of the place before anyone said a word.

