The base covers roughly 1,600 acres of Illinois lakefront, and generations of recruits remember the cold wind off the water — Lake Michigan doesn''t soften for anyone, regardless of the season.

Every sailor who wore the uniform passed through ground like this. That shared beginning is something no one can take away.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('portsmouth naval shipyard', 'Portsmouth Naval Shipyard', 'Portsmouth Naval Shipyard holds a strong claim as the oldest continuously operating naval shipyard in the United States, with roots stretching back to 1800, when the federal government established it on Seavey''s Island along the Piscataqua River between Maine and New Hampshire. That river''s deep, swift current made it a natural choice — ships could move in and out even in difficult conditions, and the surrounding forests once offered abundant timber for construction.

Over more than two centuries, the yard built and repaired vessels through virtually every conflict the Navy has fought. It became especially renowned for submarine work, producing dozens of boats from World War I onward and earning a reputation for quality that the submarine community still recognizes. During World War II, the yard ran around the clock, launching fleet submarines at a pace that helped turn the tide in the Pacific.

The place has a particular texture — granite, tidewater cold, the smell of salt and metal, and the sound of work that never quite stops. It is a working yard in the truest sense, shaped by generations of skilled hands in uniform and out.

Those who served and worked at Portsmouth helped keep the Navy''s edge sharp, one hull at a time.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('norfolk naval shipyard', 'Norfolk Naval Shipyard', 'Norfolk Naval Shipyard sits on the Elizabeth River in Portsmouth, Virginia — not Norfolk, despite the name — and holds the distinction of being the oldest and largest naval shipyard in the United States. It traces its origins to 1767, when it was established as a British colonial facility, and it has been in continuous operation ever since, surviving the Revolution, a Confederate occupation and partial burning during the Civil War, and every major conflict of the twentieth century.

