  ('pearl harbor naval shipyard', 'Pearl Harbor Naval Shipyard', 'Pearl Harbor Naval Shipyard sits on the southern shore of Oahu, Hawaii, on a harbor that Native Hawaiians called Wai Momi — "Waters of Pearl" — for the oysters that once thrived there. The United States gained access to the harbor in 1887 and established a naval station in the early twentieth century, with the shipyard formally growing into one of the Pacific Fleet''s primary repair and support facilities. Its deep anchorage and central Pacific position made it indispensable from the start.

The shipyard''s defining moment came on December 7, 1941, when the Japanese attack left the harbor in flames and pulled the United States into World War II. The workers and sailors who remained at their posts that morning — many under direct fire — began damage control and salvage operations almost immediately. The shipyard''s crews eventually raised and repaired several of the battleships sunk that day, returning them to the fight.

