  ('mare island naval shipyard', 'Mare Island Naval Shipyard', 'Mare Island Naval Shipyard carries the distinction of being the first U.S. Navy shore installation on the Pacific Coast, established in 1854 on a narrow strip of land along the Napa River in northern California. It was named, according to local legend, after a white mare belonging to General Mariano Vallejo that swam to the island after a shipwreck — and was found there alive. Commander David Farragut, who would later become America''s first full admiral, served as its first commandant.

