Those who served and worked at Hunters Point were part of the industrial backbone that kept the fleet fighting.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('long beach naval shipyard', 'Long Beach Naval Shipyard', 'Long Beach Naval Shipyard traces its origins to the early twentieth century, when the Navy recognized Southern California''s deep-water harbor as a strategic asset on the Pacific coast. The facility grew substantially during World War II, when the demand for ship construction, repair, and overhaul became relentless. At its wartime peak, tens of thousands of workers — civilian and military alike — kept the yards running around the clock to return damaged vessels to the fight.

