  ('nas oceana', 'NAS Oceana', 'Naval Air Station Oceana sits in Virginia Beach, Virginia, and traces its origins to 1940, when the Navy established an auxiliary airfield to support the growing demands of pre-war aviation training. Named for the surrounding Oceana community, it grew rapidly after Pearl Harbor and never really stopped growing. Over the decades it became one of the Navy''s premier master jet bases, home to generations of tactical aircraft — from early jets through the F-8 Crusader, the F-14 Tomcat, and eventually the F/A-18 Super Hornet squadrons that fly from its runways today.

