  ('dyess afb', 'Dyess AFB', 'Dyess Air Force Base sits on the high plains west of Abilene, Texas, and traces its roots to World War II, when it opened in 1942 as Tye Army Airfield, a training ground for pilots learning to fly in demanding conditions. It was renamed in 1956 to honor Lieutenant Colonel William Edwin Dyess, an Abilene native who survived the Bataan Death March only to die in a P-38 training accident in 1943 — a man whose story of endurance became legend before he ever made it home.

