  ('nellis afb', 'Nellis AFB', 'Nellis Air Force Base sits northeast of Las Vegas, Nevada, on ground that first became a military airfield in 1941, when the Army Air Forces needed open desert skies and unobstructed range to train aerial gunners. It was named for William Harrell Nellis, a Las Vegas-born P-47 pilot killed over Luxembourg in December 1944 — a local son honored on the base he never knew existed.

