  ('davis-monthan afb', 'Davis-Monthan AFB', 'Davis-Monthan Air Force Base sits on the high desert floor southeast of Tucson, Arizona, and its roots go back to 1919, when the city of Tucson established a municipal airfield on the site. It was renamed to honor two Tucson aviators — Lieutenant Samuel H. Davis and Second Lieutenant Oscar Monthan — both of whom died in separate aviation accidents during the 1920s. The Army Air Corps took over the field in the early days of World War II, and it has been an active military installation ever since.

