  ('edwards afb', 'Edwards AFB', 'Edwards Air Force Base sits in the high desert of California''s Antelope Valley, on the dry lakebed of Rogers Dry Lake — one of the largest natural runways in the world, stretching for miles of flat, unforgiving hardpan. The base traces its origins to the 1930s, when the Army Air Corps recognized the lakebed''s potential for flight testing. It was named for Captain Glen Edwards, a test pilot killed in 1948 while flying the experimental YB-49 flying wing.

