  ('beale afb', 'Beale AFB', 'Beale Air Force Base sits in the Sacramento Valley of northern California, about 40 miles north of Sacramento. It was established in 1942 as Camp Beale, a sprawling Army training and separation center, and was named for Edward Fitzgerald Beale — a remarkable 19th-century figure who served as a Naval officer, frontier scout, and Superintendent of Indian Affairs for California and Nevada. The base transitioned to Air Force control after World War II and found its enduring identity during the Cold War.

