  ('hill afb', 'Hill AFB', 'Hill Air Force Base sits in northern Utah, tucked between the Wasatch Mountains and the Great Salt Lake, and its story begins in 1940 when the Army Air Corps established it as a major logistics and maintenance hub. It was named for Ployer Peter Hill, a test pilot killed in 1935 while flying the prototype B-17 Flying Fortress — a fitting tribute, given how central the base would become to keeping American airpower in the fight.

