  ('mountain home afb', 'Mountain Home AFB', 'Mountain Home Air Force Base sits in the high desert of southwestern Idaho, about fifty miles southeast of Boise, on terrain that is flat, stark, and enormous — the kind of landscape that makes jet noise feel right at home. The base traces its origins to World War II, when the Army Air Forces established a training field there in 1943, taking advantage of the open skies and vast uninhabited ranges that still define the region today.

