  ('fairchild afb', 'Fairchild AFB', 'Fairchild Air Force Base sits just west of Spokane, Washington, on the dry eastern slope of the Cascades. The base was established in 1942 as a Army Air Forces installation and later renamed to honor General Muir S. Fairchild, a Washington native and distinguished airpower theorist who rose to serve as Vice Chief of Staff of the Air Force. The land itself is high desert plateau — open, windswept, and cold in winter — the kind of place that leaves an impression.

