  ('offutt afb', 'Offutt AFB', 'Offutt Air Force Base sits just south of Omaha, Nebraska, on ground that has hosted American military aviation since 1918, when it opened as Fort Crook''s balloon and airship facility. It was renamed in 1924 to honor First Lieutenant Jarvis Jennes Offutt, a Nebraska native and one of the earliest American aviators killed in World War I. That act of remembrance set the tone for a base that has always carried a weight larger than its geography suggests.

