  ('ellsworth afb', 'Ellsworth AFB', 'Ellsworth Air Force Base sits on the high plains of western South Dakota, near Rapid City, and traces its roots to World War II. Established in 1942 as Rapid City Army Air Base, it was renamed in 1953 to honor Brigadier General Richard Ellsworth, a respected officer killed in the crash of a reconnaissance aircraft that same year. The base passed from Army Air Forces control to the newly independent Air Force and quickly found its identity in the nuclear age.

