  ('scott afb', 'Scott AFB', 'Scott Air Force Base sits on the Illinois flatlands east of St. Louis, established in 1917 as one of the Army''s first aviation training fields. It was named for Corporal Frank S. Scott, a soldier who died in a 1912 aircraft accident at College Park, Maryland — making him one of the first enlisted men in American history to die in a military aircraft. That distinction gave the base a fitting identity: a place defined from the start by the human cost and promise of flight.

