  ('wright-patterson afb', 'Wright-Patterson AFB', 'Wright-Patterson Air Force Base sits outside Dayton, Ohio, on ground that has been central to American aviation almost from the moment powered flight began. The installation traces its roots to the early twentieth century, when two adjacent fields — Wright Field and Patterson Field — were named to honor the Wright brothers and First Lieutenant Frank Patterson, a Dayton native and test pilot killed in a 1918 training accident. The two fields were formally merged in 1948, the same year the Air Force became an independent service.

