  ('langley afb', 'Langley AFB', 'Langley Air Force Base sits at the tip of the Virginia Peninsula, where the James and York Rivers meet the Chesapeake Bay — a piece of ground that has seen American military aviation almost from its very beginning. Established in 1917, it was named for Samuel Pierpont Langley, a pioneering aeronautical scientist whose early experiments in flight predated the Wright Brothers. It holds the distinction of being the oldest continuously active military air installation in the United States.

