  ('seymour johnson afb', 'Seymour Johnson AFB', 'Seymour Johnson Air Force Base sits in Goldsboro, North Carolina, a community that has grown up alongside it since the base was established in 1942. It was named for Seymour A. Johnson, a Goldsboro native and Navy aviator who died in a training accident shortly before World War II. The Army Air Forces opened the field to train pilots during that war, and it has been a working military installation ever since.

