  ('robins afb', 'Robins AFB', 'Robins Air Force Base traces its roots to World War II, when the Army Air Forces established a major air depot in central Georgia to support the massive logistical demands of a nation at war. It was named for Brigadier General Augustine Warner Robins, a pioneering figure in Army Air Corps supply and logistics who died in 1940 and is widely credited with shaping the modern concept of air materiel management.

