  ('moody afb', 'Moody AFB', 'Moody Air Force Base traces its roots to World War II, when the Army Air Forces established a pilot training field outside Valdosta, Georgia, in 1941. It was named for Major George P. Moody, a Valdosta native and Army aviator killed in a 1941 training accident — one of many young men lost before the fighting even began. The base trained thousands of pilots during the war and was reactivated during the Korean War era as the Air Force expanded rapidly.

