  ('hurlburt field', 'Hurlburt Field', 'Hurlburt Field traces its roots to World War II, when it opened as an auxiliary airfield supporting Eglin Air Force Base on Florida''s Gulf Coast. It was named for Lieutenant Donald W. Hurlburt, a pilot killed in a crash there in 1943. What began as a modest training strip grew into something far more specialized over the following decades.

