  ('eglin afb', 'Eglin AFB', 'Eglin Air Force Base sits on the Florida Panhandle, straddling Okaloosa and Santa Rosa counties along the Gulf Coast. The installation traces its roots to 1935, when the Army Air Corps established a testing facility at Valparaiso, Florida. It was named for Lieutenant Colonel Frederick Eglin, a World War I aviator killed in a 1937 training accident. What began as a modest gunnery range grew into the largest air force base in the world by land area — nearly 700 square miles of pine flatwoods, swamps, and coastal waterways.

