  ('macdill afb', 'MacDill AFB', 'MacDill Air Force Base sits on a narrow peninsula jutting into Tampa Bay, a location chosen in the late 1930s precisely because the surrounding water gave pilots natural boundaries for training and the mild Florida climate allowed year-round flying. It was named for Colonel Leslie MacDill, a Army Air Corps officer killed in an aircraft accident in 1938, before the base he would lend his name to ever opened.

