  ('fort greely', 'Fort Greely', 'Fort Greely sits in Alaska''s Tanana Valley, about a hundred miles southeast of Fairbanks, where the Alaska Range rises to the south and the boreal forest stretches in every direction. It was established in 1942 as a cold-weather training and testing installation, part of the broader military buildup that made Alaska a strategic priority during World War II. It was named for Major General Adolphus Greely, a Signal Corps officer and Arctic explorer who led a harrowing scientific expedition to the high Arctic in the 1880s — a man who understood hardship in the cold better than almost anyone.

