  ('umatilla chemical depot', 'Umatilla Chemical Depot', 'Umatilla Chemical Depot sits on the high desert plateau of northeastern Oregon, established during World War II to store munitions and chemical weapons away from vulnerable coastlines and population centers. The site takes its name from the Umatilla River and the Umatilla people who have called that corner of the Columbia Basin home for generations. Its remote location — dry, windy, and vast — made it exactly what the Army needed: a place to hold dangerous material safely apart from the world.

