  ('joint base andrews', 'Joint Base Andrews', 'Joint Base Andrews sits on the outskirts of Washington, D.C., in Prince George''s County, Maryland, on ground that became a military airfield in 1942. It was named for Lieutenant General Frank M. Andrews, a pioneer of American airpower who was killed in a aircraft accident in Iceland that year while serving in World War II. What began as a wartime Army Air Forces installation grew into one of the most strategically significant air bases in the world.

