  ('mcguire afb (jb mcguire-dix-lakehurst)', 'McGuire AFB (JB McGuire-Dix-Lakehurst)', 'McGuire Air Force Base traces its origins to the 1930s, when the Army established Wrightstown Field on the pine barrens of central New Jersey. It was renamed in 1948 to honor Brigadier General Thomas B. McGuire Jr., a World War II ace who flew P-38s in the Pacific and became the second-highest-scoring American fighter pilot of the war, killed in action over the Philippines in January 1945. The base''s location — close to the Atlantic coast and major East Coast ports — made it a natural hub for rapid global deployment.

