  ('joint base charleston', 'Joint Base Charleston', 'Joint Base Charleston traces its roots to the early twentieth century, when the Navy established a presence along the South Carolina coast to defend the Atlantic seaboard. The air installation grew substantially during World War II as a hub for training and logistics, and the sprawling naval complex alongside it became one of the East Coast''s most important shipbuilding and submarine support facilities. The two installations formally merged in 2010 under the Base Realignment and Closure process, bringing Air Force and Navy missions under one command.

