  ('vandenberg space force base', 'Vandenberg Space Force Base', 'Vandenberg Space Force Base sits on California''s central coast, where the Santa Ynez Mountains meet the Pacific. The land was originally Camp Cooke, established in 1941 as an Army training ground for armored and infantry units preparing for World War II. In 1957 the Air Force took over and renamed it in honor of General Hoyt S. Vandenberg, the second Chief of Staff of the Air Force and an early champion of American air power.

