  ('buckley space force base', 'Buckley Space Force Base', 'Buckley Space Force Base sits on the high plains east of Aurora, Colorado, at roughly 5,700 feet — close enough to Denver to see the city lights, far enough to feel the full sweep of the Front Range sky. The installation traces its roots to 1942, when it was established as a Naval Air Station and named for John Harold Buckley, a Colorado native and Navy aviator killed in World War I. The Army Air Forces later shared the ground, and the installation shifted missions and designations several times before finding its modern identity as a hub for space and intelligence work.

