  ('cheyenne mountain space force station', 'Cheyenne Mountain Space Force Station', 'Cheyenne Mountain Space Force Station sits inside a granite peak in the Colorado Rockies, just southwest of Colorado Springs. Construction began in the early 1960s at the height of the Cold War, when the United States needed a command center hardened against nuclear blast and electromagnetic pulse. Engineers hollowed out roughly 200,000 tons of rock to create a complex of fifteen freestanding steel buildings — each mounted on enormous springs to absorb shockwave energy — buried under nearly 2,000 feet of solid mountain.

