  ('march arb', 'March ARB', 'March Air Reserve Base sits in the Inland Empire of Southern California, east of Los Angeles near Riverside, on ground that has been in continuous military use longer than almost any other air installation in the country. It was established in 1918 and named for Second Lieutenant Peyton C. March Jr., a young Army aviator killed in a training accident — one of the first of many airmen this place would send into the sky.

