  ('grissom afb', 'Grissom AFB', 'Grissom Air Reserve Base sits in the flat farmland of north-central Indiana, about halfway between Indianapolis and South Bend. The installation traces its roots to World War II, when it was built as Bunker Hill Naval Air Station in 1942 to train naval aviators. After the war it transferred to the Air Force, and in 1968 it was renamed in honor of Virgil "Gus" Grissom — the Indiana-born astronaut and Air Force pilot who flew in the Mercury and Gemini programs before his death in the Apollo 1 fire in 1967.

