  ('nas patuxent river', 'NAS Patuxent River', 'Naval Air Station Patuxent River sits at the confluence of the Patuxent River and the Chesapeake Bay in southern Maryland, and it has been the beating heart of American naval aviation development since it was established in 1943. The Navy chose the site deliberately — its protected waters, wide-open airspace, and distance from congested East Coast corridors made it ideal for the kind of experimental work that couldn''t be done anywhere else.

"Pax River," as anyone who served there knows it, is home to the Naval Air Warfare Center Aircraft Division and the U.S. Naval Test Pilot School — the place where the aircraft that define American sea power are pushed to their limits before they ever reach the fleet. Generations of test pilots, flight test engineers, and support personnel have wrung out everything from Cold War jets to the F/A-18 to the F-35, writing the specifications that keep carrier aviators alive.

The base sprawls across more than 6,500 acres of Maryland tidewater country — a working waterfront, long runways, and the particular quiet of a place where serious people do serious work far from the headlines.

Those who served at Pax River helped determine what American airpower could do — and that is no small thing.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('naval station newport', 'Naval Station Newport', 'Naval Station Newport traces its roots to the mid-nineteenth century, when the Navy recognized Aquidneck Island''s deep, sheltered harbor as one of the finest anchorages on the Atlantic coast. The installation was formally established in 1869, and Newport quickly became home to the Naval Torpedo Station — a pioneering center for weapons development that shaped American naval warfare for decades.

Through two World Wars, Korea, Vietnam, and beyond, Newport served as both a training hub and a strategic asset. It is perhaps best known as the home of the Naval War College, founded in 1884 and the oldest institution of its kind in the world — where generations of officers have wrestled with strategy, command, and the art of naval leadership. The Officer Candidate School and various surface warfare training commands have also called Newport home.

The character of the place is inseparable from the sea. Narragansett Bay stretches wide and cold around the island, and the wind off the water reminds you daily that you''re in the business of the ocean.

Those who studied, trained, or served at Newport carry with them something rare — a place where the Navy has thought seriously about its purpose for over 150 years.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('naval weapons station earle', 'Naval Weapons Station Earle', 'Naval Weapons Station Earle sits along the Raritan Bay in Colts Neck and Leonardo, New Jersey, established in the early 1940s to meet the urgent ammunition demands of World War II. It was named for Rear Admiral Ralph Earle, a distinguished naval ordnance officer who served as Chief of the Bureau of Ordnance during World War I. The station''s deep-water pier — one of the longest in the world at over a mile — was purpose-built to load ammunition directly onto combat vessels heading to sea.

Through every major conflict since, Earle has been the Atlantic Fleet''s primary ammunition and weapons loading station. Ships deploying to Korea, Vietnam, the Persian Gulf, and beyond were armed here. The station''s two geographically separate sections — the waterfront at Leonardo and the vast inland storage area in Colts Neck — reflect the deliberate, safety-conscious design that defines ordnance handling at this scale.

Working Earle meant long shifts, strict protocols, and the quiet knowledge that the mission you were completing mattered before the first shot was ever fired.

Those who served here kept the Fleet armed and ready — a foundational act of trust that never made headlines but never needed to.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('naval weapons station yorktown', 'Naval Weapons Station Yorktown', 'Naval Weapons Station Yorktown sits on a peninsula in Virginia where the York River meets Chesapeake Bay country — ground that has seen American military presence in one form or another since the colonial era. The installation was established in 1918, during World War I, as a naval mine depot, chosen for its deep-water access and its relative isolation from populated centers — practical virtues for a facility handling explosives.

Through World War II, the station became a critical hub for storing, assembling, and shipping ordnance to fleets operating across both oceans. Ammunition trains and barges moved through constantly, and the sheer scale of the operation made Yorktown one of the Navy''s most important logistics installations on the East Coast. In later decades it supported operations through Korea, Vietnam, and beyond, adapting its mission as weapons technology evolved.

The station shares its name with one of the most consequential pieces of ground in American history — the site where Cornwallis surrendered in 1781, just miles away. Sailors and civilians who worked here did so quietly, without fanfare, keeping the fleet armed and ready so others could do their jobs.

Those who served at Yorktown kept the magazine full — and that made everything else possible.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('nas corpus christi', 'NAS Corpus Christi', 'Naval Air Station Corpus Christi was established in 1941 on the south Texas coast, commissioned just months before the United States entered World War II. The Navy chose the site for its favorable flying weather — clear skies, steady Gulf winds, and wide open airspace that made it nearly ideal for training new aviators. The station was named for the city, which takes its own name from the Latin for "Body of Christ," given by Spanish explorers to the bay they entered on that feast day in 1519.

From the beginning, Corpus Christi''s primary calling was turning civilians into naval aviators, and it answered that call across generations. During World War II alone, the station trained tens of thousands of pilots, including a young George H.W. Bush. Through Korea, Vietnam, and beyond, NAS Corpus Christi remained one of the Navy''s most important primary and advanced flight training hubs, and it continues that mission today under Naval Aviation Training Command.

The Gulf air is humid and unforgiving in summer, and student pilots who earned their wings there did so through real work — long hours in the pattern, night flights over dark water, and the particular discipline that naval aviation demands.

Those who trained or served at Corpus Christi helped build the backbone of American air power, one aviator at a time.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('nas kingsville', 'NAS Kingsville', 'Naval Air Station Kingsville sits in the brush country of South Texas, about forty miles southwest of Corpus Christi. The Navy established the station in 1942, standing up a training base to meet the enormous demand for naval aviators during World War II. After the war it briefly reduced operations, but the Cold War brought it back to full life, and Kingsville has remained one of the Navy''s premier jet pilot training installations ever since.

The station is home to Training Air Wing Two, and its mission has stayed remarkably focused: turning student naval aviators and Marine Corps pilots into carrier-qualified jet fliers. Generations of aviators have earned their wings of gold here flying advanced jet trainers over the flat South Texas plains, where the wide-open airspace and reliable flying weather make it one of the best places in the country to learn to fly fast. The T-45 Goshawk became the backbone of that training pipeline in the 1990s and remains so today.

Kingsville is not a glamorous posting, but pilots who trained there remember the intensity, the heat, and the moment they finally greased a carrier landing — skills built right there over those scrubby South Texas fields.

Those who trained or served at Kingsville handed something forward to every squadron they ever joined.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('nas meridian', 'NAS Meridian', 'Naval Air Station Meridian sits in the pine hills of east-central Mississippi, a place that has shaped naval aviators for more than six decades. The station was established in 1961 to meet the growing demand for jet pilot training during the Cold War, when the Navy needed room to expand beyond crowded coastal fields. It was formally named McCain Field in honor of Admiral John S. McCain Sr., a distinguished naval aviator and commander of carrier forces in the Pacific during World War II — grandfather to the senator and POW who would later become famous in his own right.

The station''s primary mission has always been training, and it has remained one of the Navy''s busiest intermediate jet training bases. Generations of student naval aviators and Marine Corps pilots have earned their wings — or built toward them — flying over Mississippi''s red-clay countryside. The T-2 Buckeye and later the T-45 Goshawk became the defining aircraft of the flight line here, their engines a constant presence over the surrounding towns.

The terrain is unassuming, but what happened in the air above it was anything but — thousands of aviators carried what they learned at Meridian into combat and careers across the fleet.

Those who trained or served at NAS Meridian helped keep American air power sharp, one flight at a time.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('nsa mid-south (millington)', 'NSA Mid-South (Millington)', 'NSA Mid-South traces its roots to 1917, when the Navy established a training station on the outskirts of Millington, Tennessee — then a small town north of Memphis chosen partly for its distance from coastal vulnerabilities and its good rail connections. Over the decades it grew into one of the Navy''s most important shore installations in the interior of the country, eventually becoming home to the Bureau of Naval Personnel and the Navy''s centralized manpower and records functions.

For generations, this installation has been less about ships and more about people — the administrative and personnel backbone of the entire naval service. Sailors passed through Millington to have their careers shaped, their records kept, and their assignments cut. The Naval Aviation Technical Training Center also operated here for years, making it a hub for aviation ratings moving through the pipeline.

The base sits in the rolling terrain of west Tennessee, far from any ocean, yet it has touched virtually every sailor who wore the uniform in the modern era. Its work was quieter than a flight deck or a forward base, but no less essential — the Navy runs on people, and Millington helped keep track of every one of them.

Those who served here kept the force whole from the inside out.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('nas cecil field', 'NAS Cecil Field', 'NAS Cecil Field traces its roots to World War II, when the Navy established it in 1941 on the flat, pine-studded land west of Jacksonville, Florida. It was named for Commander Henry Barton Cecil, a naval aviator who died in a training accident in 1932 — one of countless men whose sacrifice shaped the institution before the big wars even began.

For decades Cecil Field was the Atlantic Fleet''s premier master jet base, home to fighter and attack squadrons that deployed to every major conflict from Korea through the Cold War and into the Gulf War. A-4 Skyhawks, A-7 Corsairs, and F/A-18 Hornets all flew from its runways. The base was especially known as a hub for light attack aviation, and its squadrons rotated through Mediterranean and North Atlantic deployments with steady professionalism.

The terrain is unremarkable — inland Florida, hot and humid — but the flying was anything but. Pilots trained hard here, and the operational tempo was real. When Cecil Field closed as an active installation in 1999 under BRAC, it marked the end of a long chapter in East Coast naval aviation.

Those who flew and served here carried a demanding tradition with quiet pride.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('nas key west (boca chica)', 'NAS Key West (Boca Chica)', 'Naval Air Station Key West, anchored at Boca Chica Key on the southern tip of Florida, has been a fixture of American naval aviation since World War II. The station took shape in the early 1940s as the Navy recognized the strategic value of the Florida Straits — a narrow corridor where the Atlantic meets the Gulf of Mexico — and needed airfields to patrol it. Boca Chica Field was commissioned to support anti-submarine operations as German U-boats preyed on Allied shipping in those waters.

After the war, NAS Key West evolved into one of the Navy''s premier tactical air combat training installations. Its location made it ideal year-round flying country — clear skies, open water, and restricted airspace with room to maneuver hard. Fighter pilots from every generation came through to sharpen their skills, and during the Cuban Missile Crisis of 1962, the station surged to the front lines of American readiness, with reconnaissance and patrol aircraft flying constant missions just ninety miles from Cuba.

Today it remains the home of the Naval Air Warfare Center Weapons Division''s detachment and continues to host fleet squadrons sharpening their edge.

Those who flew or served here did so at the sharp end of the hemisphere''s defense, and the ground they stood on earned its place in American history.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('nas whiting field', 'NAS Whiting Field', 'NAS Whiting Field sits in the pine flatlands north of Milton, Florida, established in 1943 to meet the enormous demand for naval aviators during World War II. It was named for Captain Kenneth Whiting, one of the U.S. Navy''s earliest aviation pioneers and a driving force behind the construction of the first American aircraft carrier.

For more than eight decades, Whiting Field has served as the Navy''s primary hub for primary flight training, producing more naval aviators than any other installation in the country. Student pilots from the Navy, Marine Corps, Coast Guard, and allied nations have all earned their wings here, learning on turboprop trainers over the Gulf Coast countryside. The field operates two main complexes — North and South — and on any given day the skies above Milton are filled with the steady hum of training aircraft flying pattern after pattern.

The pace is relentless by design: Whiting exists to turn motivated young people into aviators, and it does that job with quiet, consistent excellence year after year.

Those who trained or served here were part of something that reaches into every corner of American naval aviation — their work put wings on generations.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('nas brunswick', 'NAS Brunswick', 'NAS Brunswick traces its roots to World War II, when the Navy established an air station on the coast of Maine to help patrol the North Atlantic against German submarines threatening Allied shipping lanes. The base was commissioned in 1943, built on ground that had served as a civilian airport, and its position on Casco Bay made it a natural outpost for maritime operations reaching deep into the Atlantic.

Over the decades that followed, Brunswick became one of the Navy''s premier patrol aviation hubs. It was long home to P-3 Orion crews who flew grueling over-water missions tracking Soviet submarines during the Cold War — quiet, unglamorous work that demanded extraordinary skill and concentration. The squadrons based there logged millions of hours of ocean surveillance, often without recognition, but always with consequence.

Maine''s winters were unforgiving, and the cold gray Atlantic stretching beyond the runway was a daily reminder of what the mission demanded. At its peak, Brunswick supported thousands of military and civilian personnel and anchored the economy and identity of the midcoast region.

The station closed in 2011 under BRAC, but the professionalism of everyone who flew and served there left a mark that outlasted the mission.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('nas glenview', 'NAS Glenview', 'NAS Glenview traces its roots to the 1920s, when the site north of Chicago was developed as a private airfield. The Navy took it over in the early 1940s and transformed it into a major naval air station, using its location in the heart of the Midwest to train the enormous numbers of aviators the war demanded. It became one of the primary centers for training carrier pilots, and the fields and skies above the Illinois prairie were busy from the earliest days of American involvement in World War II straight through the decades that followed.

The station was home to Naval Air Reserve forces for much of the Cold War era, keeping trained squadrons ready long after the wartime urgency faded. The distinctive pair of massive blimp hangars — two of the largest wooden structures ever built — defined the skyline for miles and gave the base an unmistakable character that anyone who served there would recognize instantly. Those hangars once sheltered the Navy''s lighter-than-air craft and became landmarks long after that mission ended.

NAS Glenview closed in 1995 under the BRAC process, but the ground it occupied shaped generations of naval aviators and reservists who carried that training into service around the world.

Those who flew and served here helped write the story of American naval aviation, and that is no small thing.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('nas north island (naval base coronado)', 'NAS North Island (Naval Base Coronado)', 'NAS North Island traces its roots to 1917, when the Navy established an air station on the northern tip of Coronado Peninsula in San Diego Bay. The site was chosen for its geography — a broad, flat expanse of land flanked by calm harbor waters and open ocean — ideal for the early aviators who were still figuring out how to take off and land on water and on wheels both. Glenn Curtiss had already been experimenting there years before the Navy arrived, making North Island one of the genuine birthplaces of American naval aviation.

Over the following century, the station grew into one of the busiest and most consequential naval aviation hubs in the world. Carrier air wings have deployed from here through every major conflict since World War I. The aircraft carriers that call Naval Base Coronado home have projected American power across the Pacific and beyond, and the base remains a cornerstone of the Pacific Fleet today.

North Island also sits alongside the Naval Special Warfare Command, placing it at the center of some of the most demanding training pipelines in the U.S. military. The sight of carriers moored against that Coronado skyline is one of the iconic images of American sea power.

Those who served on this peninsula stood at the very origin point of naval aviation — and carried that legacy forward with every flight.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('nas point mugu', 'NAS Point Mugu', 'Naval Air Station Point Mugu sits on the Southern California coast about fifty miles northwest of Los Angeles, where the Santa Monica Mountains meet the Pacific. The Navy established the station in 1946, built on wartime momentum — the site had been used during World War II as a naval facility — and it grew quickly into one of the nation''s premier centers for missile testing and development. Its name comes from the Chumash word for "beach" or "headland," honoring the people who lived along this stretch of coast long before any uniform arrived.

Point Mugu became home to the Pacific Missile Test Center, and for decades its vast overwater range made it the ideal place to develop and evaluate the weapons systems that defined American naval aviation — from early surface-to-air missiles through the sophisticated systems of the Cold War and beyond. Squadrons and contractors cycled through constantly, and the station also supported fleet readiness training and electronic warfare work. The landscape is striking: wide tidal wetlands on one side, open ocean on the other, with the Channel Islands visible on a clear day.

Those who served at Point Mugu helped shape the edge of what American airpower could do.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('nas moffett field', 'NAS Moffett Field', 'Naval Air Station Moffett Field sits at the southern end of San Francisco Bay, in what is now Silicon Valley — a location chosen in the early 1930s precisely because its calm waters and reliable winds made it ideal for the Navy''s ambitious lighter-than-air program. The station was commissioned in 1933 and named for Rear Admiral William A. Moffett, the father of American naval aviation, who had died that same year aboard the airship *Akron* when it went down in a storm off New Jersey.

The installation was built around Hangar One, an enormous structure designed to shelter the *USS Macon*, one of the Navy''s flying aircraft carriers — rigid airships that could launch and recover biplane fighters in flight. When the lighter-than-air era ended, Moffett pivoted seamlessly to fixed-wing and later rotary-wing aviation, supporting patrol and anti-submarine operations through World War II, the Cold War, and beyond. The Navy''s P-3 Orion crews who hunted submarines across the Pacific flew from this field for decades.

NASA assumed control of much of the station in the 1990s, though the California Air National Guard continues to operate there today.

Those who served at Moffett stood watch over one of the most consequential coastlines in the world — and kept it quiet, which is exactly the point.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('treasure island naval station', 'Treasure Island Naval Station', 'Treasure Island Naval Station sits on a man-made island in San Francisco Bay, built from dredged fill and opened in 1939 as the site of the Golden Gate International Exposition. The Navy acquired it shortly after the fair closed, and for decades it served as one of the Pacific Fleet''s most active shore installations — a natural staging point for the vast campaigns of World War II and everything that followed.

During the war, hundreds of thousands of sailors and Marines passed through, shipping out toward the Central and South Pacific. The island later became home to the Naval Schools Command, training personnel in everything from communications to damage control. Its position made it a hub for processing, training, and administrative support across Korea, Vietnam, and the Cold War years.

What set Treasure Island apart was the view — the Bay, the bridges, the city just across the water — and the strange intimacy of an island base where the world felt both very close and very far away. For many, it was the last American soil they touched before the Pacific swallowed the horizon.

Those who served there carried the weight of that threshold with them, and they carried it well.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('nas alameda', 'NAS Alameda', 'NAS Alameda traces its roots to the late 1920s, when the Navy recognized the strategic value of San Francisco Bay as a hub for Pacific aviation. The station was formally commissioned in 1940 on land that had been dredged and filled from the bay''s mudflats, built deliberately and at scale to project American airpower westward. It was named for the city of Alameda, which in Spanish simply means "a grove of trees" — a quiet name for a place that would see anything but quiet history.

Through World War II, Korea, Vietnam, and the Cold War, Alameda served as a major fleet air station and homeport for aircraft carriers that shaped American naval history. Carrier air wings launched from here, and the piers hosted some of the most storied ships in the Pacific Fleet. The station was also a logistics and overhaul hub, keeping aircraft and squadrons ready across decades of sustained commitment.

What many remember is the scale of the place — the long flight line, the bay wind, and the carriers looming at the waterfront like small cities made of steel.

Those who served at Alameda kept the Pacific Fleet ready, and that work mattered every single day.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('naval station everett', 'Naval Station Everett', 'Naval Station Everett sits on the shores of Port Gardner Bay in Washington State, about thirty miles north of Seattle. The installation was established in the early 1990s — the Navy''s newest homeport on the West Coast at the time — built to disperse the Pacific Fleet and reduce vulnerability to a single catastrophic strike. It was a deliberate strategic choice, not an afterthought.

The station became home to a surface action group, hosting destroyers and frigates that deploy across the Pacific and beyond. Ships based here have supported operations in the Middle East, the Western Pacific, and wherever the fleet has been called. The installation also serves as a tenant command hub, with multiple Navy and Coast Guard units sharing the waterfront.

What strikes visitors is the setting — the Cascades visible to the east, Puget Sound stretching west, and working warships tied alongside in a city that genuinely embraced the Navy''s presence. It''s a young base by military standards, but it has already sent crews into harm''s way more than once.

Those who served here — ashore or underway — carried the full weight of the Pacific mission. That''s worth remembering.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('nas barbers point', 'NAS Barbers Point', 'NAS Barbers Point traces its roots to the early 1940s, when the Navy recognized the strategic value of Oahu''s southwestern shore. Commissioned in 1942 in the urgent aftermath of Pearl Harbor, it was named for Henry Barber, a British sea captain who wrecked his vessel on the nearby reef in 1796 — a name that stuck to the point of land for nearly two centuries before the Navy made it its own.

The station became a cornerstone of Pacific maritime patrol operations. For decades, P-3 Orion crews flew long, grinding overwater missions from its runways, hunting submarines during the Cold War across vast stretches of the Pacific. The installation supported multiple patrol squadrons and served as a key hub for search and rescue, reconnaissance, and fleet support across generations of naval aviation.

Barbers Point sat on Oahu''s dry, windward-sheltered leeward coast — a different world from the lush Kaneohe side of the island. The station closed in 1999 as part of the BRAC process, its land transitioning to civilian use, but its runways and the quiet confidence of those who flew from them are not forgotten.

To every sailor and aviator who stood watch or flew the long patrol from Barbers Point — your vigilance kept the Pacific quiet.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('naval station roosevelt roads', 'Naval Station Roosevelt Roads', 'Naval Station Roosevelt Roads sat on the northeastern tip of Puerto Rico, occupying a stretch of coastline where the Atlantic meets the Caribbean. The station traces its origins to the early 1940s, when the United States recognized the strategic importance of the island chain and established a major naval presence there to protect approaches to the Panama Canal and counter Axis threats in the Atlantic. It was named for President Theodore Roosevelt, whose advocacy for a strong navy shaped American sea power for generations.

Over the decades, "Roosey Roads" grew into one of the largest naval installations in the world by acreage, hosting operations across the Cold War, training exercises throughout the Caribbean, and logistics support for Atlantic Fleet operations. The adjacent waters and the nearby island range at Vieques made it a premier live-fire training ground for Navy and Marine Corps forces for more than fifty years.

Sailors and Marines who passed through remember the trade winds, the vivid blue water, and the sense of being at a serious, working installation tucked inside genuinely beautiful surroundings. The station closed in 2004, but the mission it sustained shaped generations of naval readiness.

Those who served there earned their salt on ground that mattered.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('naval station adak', 'Naval Station Adak', 'Naval Station Adak sits on one of the most remote patches of American soil — Adak Island, deep in the Aleutian Chain of Alaska, roughly halfway between Anchorage and Tokyo. The Navy established a presence there during World War II after Japanese forces seized the neighboring islands of Attu and Kiska in 1942, marking the only enemy occupation of U.S. territory during that war. Adak became a critical staging base for the Aleutian Campaign, launching air and naval operations that ultimately drove the Japanese from American soil.

Through the Cold War, the station took on new purpose as a premier submarine surveillance post, monitoring Soviet naval activity across the North Pacific. Its SOSUS hydrophone arrays and patrol aircraft made Adak one of the most strategically sensitive installations in the hemisphere, even if few outside the intelligence community knew exactly why.

The island itself is famously unforgiving — volcanic, fog-wrapped, battered by some of the worst weather on earth. Those who served there knew the wind personally. The station was decommissioned in 1997, but its decades of quiet, essential watch over the Pacific defined what unglamorous, consequential service looks like.

To everyone who stood watch at the edge of the Aleutians — your persistence in that punishing place mattered more than the world knew.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('naval station argentia', 'Naval Station Argentia', 'Naval Station Argentia sits on the Avalon Peninsula of Newfoundland, Canada, on a windswept stretch of coastline that juts into Placentia Bay. The base was established in 1941 as part of the Destroyers for Bases Agreement between the United States and Britain, which exchanged American destroyers for leases on British territory. From the start, Argentia was positioned to guard the North Atlantic — one of the most strategically vital and punishing stretches of ocean in the world.

During World War II, the station gained historic significance as the site where President Roosevelt and Prime Minister Churchill met aboard warships in August 1941, drafting the Atlantic Charter. Through the war and into the Cold War, Argentia served as a key hub for Navy patrol aviation and anti-submarine operations, with P-2 Neptunes and later P-3 Orions flying long, grinding missions over the gray North Atlantic searching for Soviet submarines. The base supported NATO commitments until its closure in 1994.

Argentia was never glamorous duty. The fog rolled in thick, the winters were long, and the ocean below the flight paths was unforgiving. The sailors and aviators who served there held the line at the edge of the world.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('mcas miramar', 'MCAS Miramar', 'Few installations carry as much aviation history as Marine Corps Air Station Miramar, perched on the mesa north of San Diego. The land was first used by the Navy in the 1930s and developed significantly during World War II as Naval Auxiliary Air Station Miramar, supporting the massive buildup of naval aviation on the West Coast. The Navy held it for decades, and from the mid-1960s through 1996 it became world-famous as the home of the Naval Fighter Weapons School — universally known as TOPGUN — where the nation''s best aviators sharpened their edge against dissimilar air threats.

When the Navy consolidated operations after the Cold War, the Marines took over in 1997, and Miramar became one of the largest Marine Corps air installations in the world. It supports fixed-wing, rotary-wing, and tiltrotor squadrons, and its units have deployed repeatedly to Iraq, Afghanistan, and across the Pacific.

The mesa itself is vast and sun-baked, ringed by the noise of jets that never quite lets you forget where you are. The flight line runs around the clock when the mission demands it.

Those who kept aircraft flying and crews ready from this high California ground earned every hour of it.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('mcas yuma', 'MCAS Yuma', 'Marine Corps Air Station Yuma sits in the Sonoran Desert of southwestern Arizona, one of the sunniest and most unforgiving flying environments on earth — which is precisely why the Marines chose it. The station traces its roots to 1928, when it began as a civilian airfield, and the military recognized its potential during World War II, developing it as a training base that could offer near-unlimited flying days. It was formally established as a Marine Corps installation in the postwar decades and has grown into one of the busiest tactical air training bases in the world.

Yuma''s central mission is producing combat-ready aviators and weapons officers. It is home to the Marine Aviation Weapons and Tactics Squadron One — MAWTS-1 — which runs the demanding Weapons and Tactics Instructor course, the Marine equivalent of Top Gun, drawing students from across the Corps and allied nations. The Barry M. Goldwater Range stretching to the south and west gives crews room to train hard and realistically, with live ordnance and low-level flying that few other stations can offer.

The heat, the dust, and the roar of jets overhead are constants here. Those who trained at Yuma carried skills that mattered when it counted most.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('mcas beaufort', 'MCAS Beaufort', 'Marine Corps Air Station Beaufort sits in the South Carolina Lowcountry, where the tidal marshes and Spanish moss have framed the sound of jet engines for generations. The station was established in 1943 as a Navy field during World War II, then expanded and transferred to the Marine Corps in the postwar years as the Corps built out its tactical aviation arm. It was formally designated MCAS Beaufort in 1960 and has been a jet base ever since.

The station became home to some of the Marine Corps'' most capable fixed-wing aviation, long associated with F/A-18 Hornet squadrons and, more recently, the F-35B. Units from Beaufort have deployed to support operations across the Middle East, the Balkans, and beyond — carrier-based and expeditionary alike. Marine Fighter Attack Training Squadron 501 has trained generations of pilots here, making Beaufort one of the Corps'' premier tactical air schoolhouses.

The Lowcountry setting is deceptively quiet on the ground, but the skies above it have been anything but. The sound of afterburners over the marsh is as much a part of this landscape as the tides.

Those who flew from Beaufort — or kept those aircraft in the air — carried a demanding mission with uncommon skill.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('mcas el toro', 'MCAS El Toro', 'Marine Corps Air Station El Toro sat in the broad flatlands of Orange County, California, its runways laid down in 1942 as the Pacific war demanded more airfields and more aviators. Named for the Spanish word for bull — a nod to the ranching land it was carved from — it grew quickly into one of the largest Marine air installations on the West Coast.

For more than five decades, El Toro served as a major hub for Marine Corps aviation, supporting operations from World War II through Korea, Vietnam, and the Gulf War. The station was home to Marine Aircraft Wing units flying everything from Corsairs to Phantoms to Hornets, and it served as a gateway for Marines and aircraft deploying across the Pacific. During the Cuban Missile Crisis, units here were placed on heightened alert alongside the rest of the country''s military.

El Toro''s airspace over the Santa Ana foothills was rarely quiet. The station closed in 1999 as part of base realignment, its land eventually converted to a county park — though the runways and hangars remained visible for years, quiet monuments to the noise and purpose that once filled them.

Those who flew from El Toro or called it home earned their place in a long and serious tradition of American air power.', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', true, now()),
  ('mcas tustin', 'MCAS Tustin', 'Marine Corps Air Station Tustin stood in the flatlands of Orange County, California, its most defining features visible for miles in every direction — two enormous wooden blimp hangars that remain among the largest wooden structures ever built. The station traces its origins to World War II, when the Navy established it in 1942 as a lighter-than-air facility, using blimps to patrol the Pacific coast for enemy submarines. Those hangars, each roughly a thousand feet long, could shelter multiple airships under one roof and created their own weather inside.

