  ('camp schwab (okinawa)', 'Camp Schwab (Okinawa)', 'Camp Schwab sits on the northeastern coast of Okinawa, in a rugged corner of the island far removed from the crowded south. It was established after World War II and named for Private First Class Albert Schwab, a Navy corpsman who gave his life on Okinawa in 1945 and was awarded the Medal of Honor for his courage under fire. The camp''s very name is a daily reminder of the sacrifice that made the American presence on the island possible.

