  ('camp hovey', 'Camp Hovey', 'Camp Hovey sits in the Dongducheon area of South Korea, roughly forty miles north of Seoul and well within sight of the terrain that funnels any ground threat from the north. The camp takes its name from General Joseph Hovey, though its roots trace to the years immediately following the Korean War armistice, when the United States committed to a long-term presence on the peninsula to anchor the defense of the Republic of Korea.

