  ('camp carroll (waegwan)', 'Camp Carroll (Waegwan)', 'Camp Carroll sits in the Gyeongbuk region of South Korea, near the town of Waegwan along the Nakdong River — ground that already carried the weight of history before the first American stake was driven. During the desperate summer of 1950, United Nations forces held a thin defensive perimeter along that river, and the fighting around Waegwan was among the bloodiest of the Korean War''s early months. The camp that eventually took shape there was named for Specialist Four Donald G. Carroll, killed in action in Vietnam in 1967 — a reminder that even installations built for one war carry the names of another''s fallen.

