  ('bitburg air base', 'Bitburg Air Base', 'Bitburg Air Base sits in the Eifel highlands of western Germany, a landscape of rolling forests and stone villages not far from the Belgian border. The base traces its origins to the 1950s, when NATO was building its forward defense architecture across Western Europe, and the United States Air Force established a permanent presence there as part of that collective commitment. It was known to the airmen stationed there simply as "Bit" — a small, tight-knit post in a cold and often fog-bound corner of the continent.

