  ('rhein-main air base (frankfurt)', 'Rhein-Main Air Base (Frankfurt)', 'Rhein-Main Air Base sat on the southern edge of Frankfurt, sharing a perimeter fence with what would become one of Europe''s busiest civilian airports. The U.S. military first occupied the field after World War II, taking over infrastructure the Luftwaffe had used, and the base became a cornerstone of American air power in Europe almost immediately. Its name reflected its geography — the confluence of the Rhine and Main rivers nearby had made Frankfurt a crossroads for centuries before any aircraft ever flew.

