  ('camp darby', 'Camp Darby', 'Camp Darby sits on the Tyrrhenian coast of Italy, tucked between the pine forests south of Livorno and the ancient shore of the Mediterranean. The installation traces its roots to the years just after World War II, when the United States established a logistics presence in Italy as part of the broader commitment to European security. It was named for Brigadier General William O. Darby, the founder of the U.S. Army Rangers, who was killed in action in northern Italy in April 1945 — just days before Germany''s surrender.

