  ('souda bay', 'Souda Bay', 'Souda Bay sits on the northwestern coast of Crete, a deep natural harbor that has drawn seafarers and strategists for centuries. The United States has maintained a naval presence there since the early Cold War era, when NATO recognized the bay''s exceptional geography — sheltered, deep enough for large warships, and positioned at the crossroads of the Mediterranean, the Aegean, and the approaches to the Middle East and North Africa.

